p=Path('outputs/research_questions_20260915');base='https://github.com/ndandnd/EVSP-DR/blob/codex/parallel-research-20260911/outputs/'
parts=[]
def para(t):parts.append('<p>'+t+'</p>')
def h(t):parts.append('<h2>'+t+'</h2>')
def table(headers,rows):parts.append('<table border="1" style="border-collapse:collapse"><tr>'+''.join('<th>'+html.escape(str(v))+'</th>' for v in headers)+'</tr>'+''.join('<tr>'+''.join('<td>'+html.escape(str(v))+'</td>' for v in row)+'</tr>' for row in rows)+'</table>')
parts.append('<h1>EVSP–DR: the research questions</h1>');para('Evidence checked 16 September, 08:50 EDT. This page contains conclusions and open questions; detailed experiment logs belong in the linked sources.')
h('1a. Can sequential CG + MIP recover GIRO’s fleet?')
para('<b>Sequential CG reaches target 31 in chains 3 and 5 under baseline covering assumptions. Each step adds one GIRO duty’s trips and retains earlier columns; this uses known duty grouping.</b>')
table(['Largest target matched','C1','C2','C3','C4','C5','C6'],[['Original one-hour MIP',26,28,31,29,26,28],['Including separate longer MIPs',30,30,31,29,31,30]])
para('<b>Actual buses found at the largest targets</b>')
table(['Target / MIP treatment','C1','C2','C3','C4','C5','C6'],[['31 / original one-hour MIP',34,32,31,40,36,32],['31 / best including completed longer MIPs',34,32,31,40,31,32],['32 / original one-hour MIP','—','—',33,'—',37,33]])
parts[-1]=parts[-1].replace('<td>31</td>', '<td style="background-color:#d9ead3">31</td>').replace('<td>40</td>', '<td style="background-color:#ea9999">40</td>').replace('<td>37</td>', '<td style="background-color:#f4cccc">37</td>')
para('— = no verified result yet. Green means the target was matched, not that global optimality or duplicate-free feasibility was proved. Longer target-32 searches are still running.')
para('Baseline: 240 kWh batteries, 240 kW charging, fee 5 per charging start, set covering; no reserve, shared charger capacity or ending-SOC floor. Largest matches are not guaranteed cutoffs. These covering fleets still need duplicate-removal validation. Saved-pool optimality is not full-model optimality.')
h('1b. Does a fresh start catch up with the accumulated time?')
para('<b>No in the completed comparison: warm 24/24, fresh 6/24.</b> Fresh CG starts with single-trip routes and receives T = the sum of earlier import + CG times through k. Both pools get the same one-hour MIP allowance.')
table(['Target k','Warm matches','Fresh matches'],[[5,'6/6','5/6'],[8,'6/6','1/6'],[10,'6/6','0/6'],[15,'6/6','0/6']])
para('The largest tested fresh match is k=8, chain 6. All fresh CGs converged before their time budget expired. Four fresh pools exclude the target; 14 misses remain unresolved. This retrospective comparison has code/hardware differences. The budget excludes intermediate MIPs; graph preparation is accounted for separately.')
h('2. Which algorithm changes worked?')
para('<b>Summer:</b> event-based time/SOC graphs allow more charging choices, and exact shortest-path pricing can certify that no improving route remains in the modeled graph. This is stronger than the older capped label search; it is not a controlled historical speedup claim.')
para('<b>Recent controlled tests:</b> indexed route replay reduced CG time 12.4–18.1%; skipping unused LP-matrix construction saved 9.3–14.6%. Keeping all inherited routes rather than 512 cut CG time 40.6–64.5% and improved the three tested fleets from 9→8, 11→10 and 17→15. These are selected-input results; percentages cannot be added. Capacity-pricing caching has not demonstrated a speedup.')
h('3. Without the start fee, does changing duties beat charging optimization alone?')
para('Yes at all three tariff peaks on this five-duty set. Fixed GIRO duties with optimized charging versus fresh CG plus duplicate cleanup: 08:00, 128.29 versus 124.69 (2.8% lower); 12:00, 164.23 versus 160.61 (2.2% lower); 18:00, 95.29 versus 88.30 (7.3% lower). Each schedule uses five buses, assigns every trip once and matches its comparator’s total ending energy. Costs use continuous replay, in tariff cost units. The evening cleanup reached its one-hour limit with a 0.136% grid-objective gap; its improvement is verified, but charging optimality is unproved.')
para('Fresh CG converged in 15.2, 16.8 and 22.2 minutes, excluding graph construction; duplicate cleanup then assigned each trip once. Settings: 240 kWh/350 kW, zero start fee, no reserve or shared capacity, and matched total ending energy—not per-bus SOC. This is an improvement on one instance under simplified physics, not proof of global charging optimality or full GIRO feasibility.')
h('Two additional questions that matter')
para('4. Where is the bottleneck? At target 31, five CG runs hit the four-hour limit; chain 5 converged in 197.5 minutes. Chain 5 then improved from 36 to 31 buses using unchanged columns and a longer MIP search: those columns were sufficient. Its pool bound is 30, so 30 versus 31 remains unresolved. Pricing certificates concern the modeled graph; MIP proofs concern the saved columns. Duplicate removal still needs validation before calling the larger fleets executable schedules.')
para('<b>5. Do the conclusions survive realistic charging constraints?</b> Reserve, station power/capacity and ending SOC remain incompletely tested together. Keep those results separate from baseline scaling. Decomposition is a possible method for scaling; broader instances and tariffs test whether the findings generalize.')
para('<b>Next: validate large-chain trip assignments; test charging savings on more instances and under stricter constraints. Historical code audits remain in the linked reports.</b>')
